  <h2 id="section-6">6. Best Practices for Compressing Images to WebP</h2>
  <p>
    To achieve the ideal balance between minuscule file weights and pristine visual appeal, apply these industry-standard compression rules:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Find the Quality Sweet Spot (75% to 82%)</h3>
  <p>
    Most users set compression sliders to 100%, assuming anything less ruins the image. In reality, the difference between 100% and 80% WebP quality is virtually imperceptible to the human eye, yet the 80% setting reduces file size by an astounding <strong>60% to 75%</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hero Banners & Fullscreen Backgrounds:</strong> Use 80% to 85% quality to prevent banding in smooth sky gradients.</li>
    <li><strong>Blog Post Illustrations & Editorial Photos:</strong> Use 75% to 80% quality for lightning-fast article rendering.</li>
    <li><strong>Thumbnail Grids & Small Icons:</strong> Use 65% to 75% quality since compression artifacts remain invisible at small dimensions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Know When to Use Lossy vs. Lossless WebP</h3>
  <p>
    Understanding compression mechanics guarantees optimal results:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use Lossy WebP:</strong> For real-world photography, nature scenery, human portraits, and complex colorful graphics. Lossy compression discards subtle imperceptible color variations to achieve maximum byte reduction.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Lossless WebP:</strong> For technical diagrams, user interface screenshots, logos with small typography, and pixel art where every line must remain mathematically exact.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Resize Dimensions Before Compressing</h3>
  <p>
    Never upload an unmodified 6000 × 4000 pixel DSLR camera photo to a website container that only displays at 1200 × 800 pixels. Scaling physical pixel dimensions down to match your site's maximum container width eliminates millions of unnecessary pixels before compression even begins.
  </p>

