  <h2 id="section-7">7. Common Image Compression Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
  <p>
    Watch out for these widespread mistakes that degrade website performance and visual quality:
  </p>

  <div class="warning-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Avoid Re-Compressing Compressed Files:</strong> Compressing an already low-quality, artifact-ridden JPEG into WebP compounds compression noise. Always compress directly from your highest-resolution source image!
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>Mistakes That Hurt Page Performance</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Over-Compressing Below 60% Quality:</strong> Pushing lossy quality sliders too low introduces noticeable color blocking, smudging, and blurry text halos.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retaining Heavy EXIF Metadata:</strong> Camera metadata (GPS coordinates, camera serials, exposure tables) adds unnecessary kilobytes to web graphics. Always strip non-essential metadata for web publishing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Using Non-sRGB Color Profiles:</strong> Images exported using Adobe RGB or CMYK color profiles render with dull or mismatched colors on standard web browsers. Always use sRGB.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skipping Responsive Image Markup:</strong> Serving desktop-sized 1920px WebP images to 375px mobile screens wastes mobile bandwidth. Pair your WebP files with HTML responsive srcset tags.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Neglecting Dimension Scaling:</strong> Relying solely on compression without resizing the physical pixel width leaves massive dimensional bloat in your files.
    </li>
  </ol>

