  </p>

  <h3>Deploying Responsive HTML Picture Tags</h3>
  <p>
    To ensure modern WebP delivery while maintaining fallback safety for specialized legacy environments, implement the standard HTML5 picture element:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Provide your primary WebP asset inside the <code>&lt;source type="image/webp"&gt;</code> tag.</li>
    <li>Include a fallback JPEG inside the standard <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag for universal backward compatibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When Not to Use WebP</h3>
  <p>
    While WebP is the premier format for web and mobile interfaces, certain non-web workflows still require alternative formats:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Commercial Physical Print:</strong> High-end magazine and billboard printing requires uncompressed 300+ DPI CMYK TIFF or PDF files.</li>
    <li><strong>Raw Photo Editing Workflows:</strong> Photographers should retain RAW, DNG, or 16-bit TIFF master files for non-destructive color grading.</li>
  </ul>

